/*!
    \page shared-security-model.html
    \title Qt Shared Security Model
    \brief Understanding security responsibilities in Qt applications.
    \ingroup security

    The Qt Shared Security Model defines the distribution of security
    responsibilities among Qt application developers, the Qt Group and
    Qt Project as providers of the Qt Framework, and operators or device
    manufacturers.
    The model establishes clear expectations regarding responsibility for
    security across the various layers of the technology stack in a typical
    Qt application or device.

    This page provides guidance for developers building Qt applications and for
    security researchers reporting security issues in Qt.

    \section1 Overview

    Security in Qt applications is a shared responsibility. Qt provides secure
    frameworks and libraries, but the ultimate security of an application
    depends on how developers use these components and how they handle
    application-specific concerns.

    A typical Qt application can be visualized as a stack:

    \image {shared-security-model.svg}{Layers of responsibility: App, Qt, Process, OS,
    Hardware}

    \section1 Roles and responsibilities

    \section2 Responsibilities of the application developer

    You, as an application developer, are responsible for the security of the
    application-specific code, assets, and configuration. You also assume
    responsibility for any standalone third-party libraries that are shipped
    with the application.

    Qt aims to provide a user-friendly, safe-by-default API. However, it is your
    responsibility to use the API diligently, and for example check for error
    conditions and handle them appropriately. For an overview of security
    considerations when using Qt, see \l{Security in Qt}.

    Review your application in terms of which data sources are trusted and which
    are untrusted. Make sure to validate and sanitize untrusted data using
    appropriate Qt APIs. See also \l{Handling Untrusted Data} for more information.

    Plan mechanisms to update the application and apply Qt security updates in a
    timely manner. Follow Qt releases and security announcements, and update Qt
    accordingly. Also, ensure that you obtain Qt from a trusted source.

    If you ship Qt as part of your application, limit the number of libraries
    and plugins to reduce the attack surface. For example, ship only the image
    plugins for the formats you actually need.

    When switching Qt defaults, be careful that you don't accidentally disable
    security features or weaken security guarantees. For example, don't disable
    SSL/TLS certificate validation unless you have a very good reason to do so.
    Another example is changes to the Qt search paths, for instance through
    \l{qt.conf}. Make sure all paths are trustworthy.

    \section2 Responsibilities of Qt

    The Qt Group and Qt Project are responsible for the security of the Qt
    framework itself, including all
    officially distributed binaries and redistributables. This includes
    maintaining and updating any third-party components bundled with Qt
    libraries and plugins, tracking known vulnerabilities, and providing timely
    fixes.

    Qt aims to provide APIs that are secure by default. Common use cases are
    designed to minimize the risk of misuse, while operations with security
    implications require explicit developer action.

    Qt provides APIs a developer can use to process and validate
    \l{Untrusted data}{data from untrusted sources}, such as user input, network
    communication, or external files. Where it is unreasonable for a developer
    to implement their own validation, Qt provides built-in validation.
    For example, Qt's image decoders are designed to securely parse
    image files. There might be APIs though where neither is possible or
    reasonable; in these cases, the documentation warns about calling the API
    with untrusted data.

    \section2 Responsibilities of operator or device manufacturer

    Both Qt and the application have to rely on the security and integrity of
    the operating environment (hardware, operating system, and
    services). On desktop systems, this
    is typically the responsibility of the operator of the application;
    for example, by keeping the operating system up-to-date.
    On embedded devices, it is the responsibility of the device manufacturer.

    This includes the \e{process environment} that the Qt application is running
    in. For instance, all desktop applications expect the directories in the
    \c{PATH} environment variable to be trustworthy.

    \section3 Assumptions

    Qt's security guarantees and the acceptance criteria below rely on the
    following baseline assumptions about the environment in which a Qt
    application runs. If any of these assumptions do not hold, the related
    issues fall outside the intended purpose and supported conditions of use
    on which Qt's cybersecurity risk assessment is based, and are therefore
    not treated as vulnerabilities in Qt.

    \list
    \li \b{The attacker does not already control the device or
        account}: Qt does not defend against an attacker who already has
        physical or remote control of the device running the application,
        or of the user account it runs under (for example, a stolen or
        unlocked device, an open remote desktop session, or a compromised
        account), independently of whether they can reach the application
        process itself. The test is whether the attacker ends up with
        privilege, access, or trust they did not already have. Where an
        unprivileged local actor gains the application's privileges, or
        influences it through data planted in a location the application
        reads, a security boundary has been crossed and the issue is in
        scope, even though the attack is local. See also
        \l{Issues Qt will not accept}.
    \li \b{The operating system and its environment are configured
        correctly}: Qt assumes the OS, its configuration, and the process
        environment (such as the \c{PATH} and \c{QT_PLUGIN_PATH} variables)
        have not been tampered with or misconfigured. See
        \l{Responsibilities of operator or device manufacturer}.
    \li \b{Exposed hardware is genuine}: Qt assumes that hardware the
        application interacts with (peripherals, sensors, or other attached
        devices) is genuine and has not been substituted or physically
        compromised. This does not extend to the data such hardware sends:
        input received from external devices is untrusted input, and
        defects reachable through it remain in scope. See
        \l{Handling Untrusted Data}.
    \endlist

    \section1 Security issue acceptance criteria

    Security researchers should use the following guidelines when determining
    whether a potential security issue should be reported to the Qt Project:

    \section2 Issues Qt will accept

    The Qt Group and the Qt Project accept security issues that:

    \list
        \li \b{Affect Qt framework code}: Vulnerabilities in Qt's own code,
            including memory corruption bugs, type confusion, use-after-free,
            buffer overflows, and similar memory safety issues in Qt libraries.
        \li \b{Affect bundled third-party components}: Security vulnerabilities
            in third-party libraries and components that are bundled with Qt.
        \li \b{Break security guarantees}: Issues where Qt APIs fail to provide
            documented security guarantees or violate reasonable security
            expectations for security-critical APIs.
        \li \b{Affect default configurations}: Security issues in default Qt
            configurations that could affect typical applications without
            developer intervention.
    \endlist

    Qt also expects security issues to have a realistic attack scenario. Issues
    that cannot be exploited in practice or require unrealistic conditions
    will be treated as defects, but not considered security issues.

    \section2 Issues Qt will not accept

    The Qt Group and Qt Project do not accept reports for security issues that:

    \list
        \li \b{Require insecure application code}: Issues that only manifest
            when the application developer writes insecure code or misuses Qt
            APIs in ways that are not in line with documentation or established
            best practices.
        \li \b{Are based on application-specific logic flaws}: Security issues in
            application-specific logic, such as authentication bypasses,
            authorization flaws, or business logic vulnerabilities.
        \li \b{Depend on malicious application code}: Issues that require the
            application to execute malicious code. Qt assumes that application
            developers control their own code and resources.
        \li \b{Require malicious QML/JavaScript}: \l{Qt Qml} assumes all QML and
            JavaScript code is provided by the application developer. Running
            untrusted QML code is not supported. See \l{Handling Untrusted Data}
            for more information.
        \li \b{Depend on issues in operating environment}: Vulnerabilities in the
            underlying operating system, hardware, or system libraries that are
            not bundled with Qt.
        \li \b{Depend on an insecure process environment}: Attacks that require
            an insecure process environment, such as untrusted directories in the
            \c{PATH} or \c{QT_PLUGIN_PATH} environment variables, are outside the
            scope of Qt's security responsibility.
    \endlist

    Furthermore, reports for cybersecurity issues need a realistic attack scenario that
    \list
        \li \b{Crosses a security boundary}: an issue crosses a security
            boundary when it gives the attacker privilege, access, or trust
            they did not already hold. For example, issues which require an
            attacker already having access to the application process and being able to
            execute code are not considered security issues, because the
            attacker already holds that access.
        \li \b{Is not based on social engineering}: Issues that primarily rely on social
            engineering or tricking users rather than technical vulnerabilities
            in Qt.
    \endlist

    \section1 Frequently asked questions

    \section2 Who is responsible for validating user input?

    The application developer is responsible for validating user input.
    While Qt provides validation tools (see \l{QValidator} and
    \l{Qt Quick Text Input Handling and Validators}{QML Validators}), it is
    the application's responsibility to use these tools appropriately and
    implement application-specific validation logic.

    \section2 Is Qt responsible for SQL injection vulnerabilities?

    No. SQL injection vulnerabilities typically result from improper use of SQL
    APIs. Developers must use parameterized queries and prepared statements
    (such as \l{QSqlQuery::prepare}{QSqlQuery::prepare}) to prevent SQL
    injection. However, Qt is responsible for ensuring that these secure APIs
    function correctly.

    \section2 What about cross-site scripting (XSS) in Qt applications?

    XSS prevention is primarily the application developer's responsibility.
    Developers must properly sanitize and escape data when displaying untrusted
    content, especially in web views or rich text components. Qt provides APIs
    like \l{QString::toHtmlEscaped} to assist with this, and \l{Qt WebEngine}
    implements web platform security features, but the application must use
    these correctly.

    \section2 Who handles encryption and secure communications?

    Both Qt and the application developer share responsibility:
    \list
        \li Qt provides secure networking APIs (\l{QSslSocket},
            \l{QNetworkAccessManager}) with secure defaults.
        \li Application developers must properly use these APIs, implement
            certificate validation, manage keys securely, and handle sensitive
            data appropriately. If they bundle OpenSSL with the application, it
            is also their responsibility to keep it up to date and apply
            security patches.
    \endlist

    \section2 Is loading untrusted QML code a security issue?

    Running untrusted QML code is explicitly not supported and is considered a
    misuse of the QML engine. \l{Qt Qml} is designed with the assumption that
    all QML and JavaScript code is trusted and provided by the application
    developer. If your application needs to load untrusted content, use
    appropriate sandboxing mechanisms or alternative approaches, like a custom
    Domain-Specific Language (DSL).

    \section2 What about image file vulnerabilities?

    Qt considers image decoding a security-critical API and takes responsibility
    for securely parsing image files from untrusted sources. Security issues in
    Qt's image decoders (buffer overflows, crashes, and similar issues) should be
    reported to the Qt Group or Qt Project. However, applications must still
    validate image sources, handle large images appropriately, and manage
    application-specific image security concerns.

    \section2 How do I report a security issue?

    See \l{Reporting Security Issues} in the \l{Security in Qt} page for
    detailed instructions on reporting security issues to the Qt Project.

    \section2 What versions of Qt receive security updates?

    Security updates are provided for supported Qt versions. See
    \l{Qt Releases} for information about supported versions and
    \l{Extended Security Maintenance} for end-of-support versions.

    \section1 Additional Resources

    For more information about security in Qt, see:

    \list
        \li \l{Security in Qt}
        \li \l{Handling Untrusted Data}
        \li \l{Application Permissions}
        \li \l{https://contribute.qt-project.org/quips/15}{QUIP 15}
            Qt Project Security Policy
        \li \l{https://wiki.qt.io/List_of_known_vulnerabilities_in_Qt_products}
            {List of Known Vulnerabilities in Qt Products}
    \endlist
*/
